-- Load with MCP database_execute after publishing. Safe to run again.
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (path, title, body) VALUES
('/', 'Littlebox overview', 'Littlebox lets a person who does not write code, and a person who does, make the same small app and take it to another machine with its accounts, its data and its rules unchanged. The code, who may open a Deployment, and who may read a row stay separate. The Validation Gate in the editor and the Kernel that serves the app refuse the same Capsule with the same words. A check that passes in the editor still passes on the machine the app leaves on. The five jobs A person who does not write code describes an app and gets one that runs. A person who writes code edits the same app''s files. Both of them hit the same checks. A query that reads rows names a LIMIT, in the editor and on the Kernel that leaves with the app. Either person can take the app to another machine with its accounts, its data and its rules. Who may open the app, and who may read a row, stay decisions of the app. The host does not replace them. How to decide what belongs A new capability belongs in the product only when the Validation Gate and the Kernel refuse it with the same words, and a package can carry the app''s accounts, data and rules. Anything else is operator work on the installation, not an App feature. After any write through MCP or the editor, observe the effect through the same door before retrying or telling the owner it worked. The rules topic of platform_guide lists the read-back for each write tool. Decide what a credential may do from the can map of session_inspect , not from memory of login flags. Start here These are the three steps of a first run. Run the commands from the Littlebox repository root. 1. Get a credential bun run littlebox -- login --dir ./my-app --url http://localhost:4173 login reads LITTLEBOX_EMAIL and LITTLEBOX_PASSWORD from the environment and saves a 24-hour token with read , write and publish in ./my-app/.littlebox/credentials.json . The CLI reference lists the other variables and flags. 2. Get an app that runs bun run littlebox -- init --dir ./my-app --name ''My app'' --url http://localhost:4173 bun run littlebox -- dev --dir ./my-app init registers the project and writes a template. dev runs the app on a local Development Kernel and republishes when the files change. To publish to the Kernel the credential names, run bun run littlebox -- publish --dir ./my-app . In the editor, the same step is an apply through the Validation Gate. The first app guide walks through it. 3. Prove it Open the Deployment URL. Call one Server Action from the page. Read the project back with project_inspect over MCP. When you are checking a contract, run conform against action-contract.json . If this path grows, the product got harder. The fix is to shorten the path, not to add a page. For agents An agent that connects over MCP calls platform_describe and projects_list first. It then reads the rules and pieces topics of platform_guide . The MCP reference lists every topic. This site publishes llms.txt with a link to each page''s source fragment. Read next Concepts , the words the rest of the docs use The Capsule , the files of an app Pick the smallest piece , which declaration fits a need Example apps'),
('/concepts', 'Concepts', 'The words an app author meets, in the order the app meets them. They are the terms of CONTEXT.md , and the editor and the Kernel use the same words in their messages. Terms the code introduced in November 2026 are written in lowercase in prose, as the editor already says them. Examples are guard, notice, schedule, package, migration and invitation. Before the app runs Project The source lineage, schema, capability configuration and deployment history owned by a workspace member. Its source authority is either Managed by Littlebox or External under its owner. Capsule The editable source of a Project taken as one unit. It is a nested source tree with an entrypoint, app.js or src/main.ts , plus optional index.html and styles.css , the Database Model in schema.ts and the Server Action declarations in actions.json . See The Capsule . Database Model The editable declaration of a Project''s intended data structure, from which schema revisions and migrations are derived. It is source authority, unlike the current database or a generated SQL snapshot. Server Action A named, deployed operation that authorizes a Runtime Actor before running SQL or recording a Signal. It is the deployed app''s only database interface. It runs one SQL statement. See Server Actions . Validation Gate The check every agent-proposed change must pass before it is applied to a Project. The Gate and the Kernel refuse the same Capsule with the same words. See The Gate and the Kernel . Build Artifact The validated, immutable static output of a Project revision that a Deployment serves. It holds no source authority, runtime database access or administrative capability. Under the Kernel''s Content Security Policy its own inline scripts and same-origin files load, and inline event handler attributes and other origins do not. Deployment The published, addressable instance of a Project with its own hostname, an optional custom domain, visibility, signup policy and database. Everything it is lives in one folder on the Kernel volume. Republishing replaces its Build Artifact and keeps its hostname and data. Public Deployment A Deployment whose application shell may be opened without signing in. A signed-out visitor may call Server Actions declared anonymous . Public visibility grants no database access and no access to private files. Private Deployment A Deployment whose application shell and runtime require a signed-in Runtime Actor before anything loads or runs. The HTTP route of an anonymous action still answers its callers. Project Owner An authenticated workspace member allowed to edit a Project and administer its data, schema, files, Usage and deployments. Runtime Actor Whoever calls a Deployment at runtime. It is an anonymous visitor, a signed-in app user, or a service holding an API key. Its permissions are evaluated by server-side actions and never imply Project ownership. Signup Policy The per-Deployment rule for who may create a Runtime Actor account. It is Open, Invitation only, Closed or Allowlist. See Accounts and access . Agent Credential A Project Owner''s revocable delegation that lets an external agent perform selected operations on selected Projects for a limited time. It grants no Runtime Actor identity. Development Kernel The local development authority that reproduces the Kernel interfaces without granting an application access to production resources. littlebox dev runs one. As the app grows Guard A mutation''s declared refusal, "guard": "<message>" . The Kernel answers it instead of { changes: 0 } when the statement changes no row because the app''s own rule in its WHERE said no. Notice The email a mutation declares with notify , queued as an email job when its statement changes a row. It never goes to an address from the input. Schedule A mutation''s declaration that the Kernel''s own timer runs it, for work that must happen when nobody asks. The mutation takes no input. Signal A named occurrence the Capsule declares on a Server Action. The Kernel records it when the action succeeds, next to Usage and apart from application rows. Only the Project Owner reads Signals. Usage The Kernel-owned daily totals for one Deployment''s authorized Runtime Interface operations. Only the Project Owner reads it. Invitation The Project Owner''s offer of an account on one Deployment to one email, sent as a seven-day Challenge. Callers The kinds of caller an HTTP route takes, any of signature , key and session . Signature Scheme How a route''s signature is computed and where it travels. littlebox is the default. hmac-body , timestamped and svix follow the providers that send them. Signing Secret A value the Project Owner sets through the Admin Interface. It never appears in actions.json , a Build Artifact or an export. API Key A secret the Project Owner issues for one Deployment so a program can call it as a Runtime Actor of kind service. It is sent as Authorization: Bearer . The Kernel stores its hash only. Call Action A Server Action of "kind": "call" that declares one HTTP request to another app or an outside service. The Kernel sends it from its own process, never from the browser. Outbox The calls a Deployment''s call actions queued, kept in calls.sqlite with their attempts and status. The Project Owner reads it with calls_read . SDK Module A namespace on window.miniapp , such as auth , actions or files , with a contract of calls, arguments, return values, refusals and limits. docs/sdk.md is rendered from it. Action Contract The generated description of a Capsule''s Server Actions, action-contract.json . It is written from actions.json and schema.sql , never edited, and travels in the export. littlebox conform checks a backend against it. Custom Domain A hostname its owner controls on which one Deployment answers next to its generated hostname. The owner proves control with a TXT record. Package The export of one Deployment''s state, which is its database, its accounts without sessions or email links, its API keys revoked at import, its files, and its Usage and Signals when it has any. A versioned manifest lists each component with its size and SHA-256. Migration The move of one Deployment to another installation so that exactly one installation takes its writes. Maintenance The state of a Deployment that takes no writes. Its host shows a page naming the operation, and its email waits. Managed Project A Project whose source and database-schema authority remain in Littlebox, so changes pass through its editor, agent, managed build and Validation Gate. External Project A Project created with external source authority or transferred out of Littlebox through Eject. The owner supplies Build Artifacts and accepts schema compatibility responsibility. Eject The irreversible transfer of a Managed Project''s source and database-schema authority to its owner, while Littlebox keeps hosting and administering it. It needs the manage_lifecycle permission. Words for running an installation These matter only to whoever operates a Kernel. CONTEXT.md defines each of them. Littlebox Kernel, Runtime Interface, Admin Interface Agent Workspace, Account, Account State, Workspace, Workspace Home Setup Key, Invitation Code, Challenge, Verification Mode Email Job, Sender Preflight, Copy, Revocation Record A copy moves an installation. A package moves one app. Packages An npm package is imported by name once the Capsule''s package.json lists it with an exact version. The editor''s build fetches it from esm.sh, littlebox build reads it from node_modules, and either way it goes into the app''s bundle. Read next The Capsule Server Actions Accounts and access Pick the smallest piece'),
('/capsule', 'The Capsule', 'A Capsule is the editable source of one Project, taken as one unit. The editor, the agent and the CLI all read and write this shape. Layout mini-app/ ├── index.html ├── about.html # every .html at the root is a page ├── 404.html # answers an unknown path under the default routing ├── pages/ # more pages, published without the prefix ├── public/ # published as they are ├── styles.css ├── app.js # or src/main.ts ├── src/ │ └── feature.ts ├── schema.ts └── actions.json This is the layout of docs/architecture.md . An app that publishes with littlebox also carries schema.sql , as the apps in examples/ do, and an External Project keeps its manifest in littlebox.json . What each file does File Role app.js or src/main.ts The entrypoint. Relative imports from it are bundled. Today a Capsule imports only its own files. index.html , other .html Pages. Every .html at the root and under pages/ is a page. Pages under pages/ publish without the prefix. styles.css Styles the pages load. schema.ts The Database Model, from which schema revisions and migrations are derived. schema.sql The schema the Kernel and the Gate parse for publication. It may only create tables and indexes, and it must be safe to run twice. actions.json The Server Action declarations. See Server Actions . 404.html The page answered for a path the Deployment does not hold, under the default routing mode. public/ Files published as they are. The sources do not agree on which file holds the schema. CONTEXT.md and docs/architecture.md name schema.ts as the Database Model. kernel-rs/src/guide.rs says to declare the schema in schema.sql for artifact publication, and docs/architecture.md says the Kernel parses actions.json and schema.sql at release_plan . The examples carry both files. Treat schema.sql as the one the Kernel reads at release. From Capsule to Build Artifact A Deployment serves the Build Artifact built from the Capsule, not the Capsule. Every builder makes it by one rule set. Each page gets the SDK injected. The files the pages load, and public/ , are included. Binary files travel as base64, named in encodings . A build that names a file the Capsule does not hold is refused. The routing mode is pages by default. A path the artifact does not hold answers 404 with its 404.html . In spa mode such a path serves index.html . The builders and the Kernel refuse any other mode with the same words. The Gate checks the schema and actions.json . A page that names a missing file passes the Gate and fails at build or release. See Named divergences . What the published page may do A published app runs under the Kernel''s Content Security Policy. Its own inline scripts and same-origin files load. Inline event handler attributes such as onclick="..." and other origins do not. Attach listeners in JavaScript and keep scripts and fetches on the app''s own origin. The editor preview applies no such policy, so code that works there can still fail once published. Who holds the source A Managed Project keeps its source in Littlebox, and changes pass through the editor and the Validation Gate. An External Project keeps its source in the owner''s folder. littlebox init creates an External Project. Eject turns a Managed Project into one, and the step cannot be undone. Read next Server Actions The Gate and the Kernel Edit files with the CLI Named divergences'),
('/server-actions', 'Server Actions', 'A Server Action is a named operation declared in actions.json . It is the deployed app''s only database interface. The browser sends the action name and its input. The SQL and the typed bindings stay on the Kernel. Shape This is the actions topic of platform_guide , as a file. { "version": 1, "actions": { "items.list": { "kind": "query", "access": "authenticated", "statement": "SELECT id,title FROM items WHERE user_id=?1 LIMIT 50", "bindings": [{ "source": "actor", "field": "id" }] }, "items.create": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "authenticated", "statement": "INSERT INTO items(title,user_id) VALUES(?1,?2)", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "title", "type": "string", "maxLength": 200 }, { "source": "actor", "field": "id" } ] } } } Bindings fill ?1 , ?2 and so on, in order. A binding takes its value from one of three sources. Source Fields Value input key , type , maxLength , nullable What the caller sent. nullable: true binds an omitted value or an explicit null as SQL NULL. actor field of id or role The caller''s ID, or the app user''s role. See Accounts and access . kernel field of now The time of the call. A fourth form, { "source": "literal", "value": ... } , binds a constant string, number or null (see src/kernel/actions.ts ). Kinds Kind Runs Keys it takes query One SELECT kind , access , statement , bindings , http mutation One INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE or REPLACE The query keys, plus signal , guard , notify and schedule signal No SQL. It records a Signal. kind , access , bindings , signal call No SQL. The Kernel sends an HTTP request. kind , access , bindings , url , method , body , headers , auth , then A key the kind does not take is refused. A misspelled notfy does not pass and do nothing. Statement rules An action runs one statement. A WITH clause sits inside the statement, never in front of it. A mutation has no RETURNING . It answers { changes, lastInsertId } . Read rows with a query. A query that reads rows names a top-level LIMIT . See The Gate and the Kernel . CREATE , ALTER and DROP are refused. Tables and indexes belong in schema.sql , and Littlebox runs no triggers or views. Access access is anonymous or authenticated . An anonymous action is open to a signed-out visitor on a Public Deployment. granted is refused. On a Private Deployment nothing loads or runs without a signed-in Runtime Actor, apart from an HTTP route that answers its callers. guard A mutation writes its rules into its WHERE . When the rule says no, the statement changes no row. A guard turns that into a refusal with the app''s own sentence. "waitlist.join": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "anonymous", "guard": "That address is already on the list.", "statement": "INSERT INTO waitlist(email,name,source,created_at) VALUES(lower(trim(?1)),?2,?3,?4) ON CONFLICT(email) DO NOTHING", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "email", "type": "string", "maxLength": 254 }, { "source": "input", "key": "name", "type": "string", "maxLength": 80 }, { "source": "input", "key": "source", "type": "string", "maxLength": 80 }, { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ] } The guard is a string of 1 to 200 characters on one line. A refusal is a tool error on the runtime endpoint and a 403 on a route. It counts under refused in Usage and records no Signal. notify A mutation may queue one email when its statement changes a row. "waitlist.join": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "anonymous", "statement": "INSERT INTO waitlist (email, source, joined_at) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3) ON CONFLICT (email) DO NOTHING", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "email", "type": "string", "maxLength": 254 }, { "source": "input", "key": "source", "type": "string", "maxLength": 40 }, { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ], "notify": { "to": "owner", "subject": "New on the waitlist from {input.source}", "body": "{input.email} joined the waitlist.\nThey came from {input.source}." } } to is owner , actor or { "column": "<name>" } . The Kernel never sends to an address from the input. Templates take {input.<key>} , {actor.id} , {kernel.now} , {changes.count} and, in the body, {changes} . The mutation''s answer never depends on the notice. CONTEXT.md describes a notice as going to the Project Owner or to the calling account. guide.rs and docs/mcp.md add the { "column" } form, which sends to each proven account of the app whose ID a changed row holds in that column. This page follows the guide. schedule A mutation that takes no input may declare that the Kernel''s timer runs it. "notes.surface": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "authenticated", "schedule": { "every": "5m" }, "statement": "UPDATE notes SET surfaced_at = ?1 WHERE surfaced_at IS NULL AND remind_at <= ?1", "bindings": [{ "source": "kernel", "field": "now" }], "signal": { "name": "notes.surfaced" } } every takes 5m to 24h . daily takes a UTC time such as 02:00 . A run has no caller, so an actor binding is null and the guard does not apply. Publishing does not run it, and the editor preview has no timer. Nothing is pushed to a page, so a page polls a query. signal A mutation may record a named occurrence for the owner when it changes a row. An action of kind signal records one and runs no SQL. "downloads.opened": { "kind": "signal", "access": "anonymous", "bindings": [{ "source": "input", "key": "fileId", "type": "string", "maxLength": 64 }], "signal": { "name": "download.opened", "properties": ["fileId", "actor"] } } Properties are input keys or actor . Only the Project Owner reads Signals, with signals_read . http An action declared anonymous may answer one method and path on its Deployment host. A query binds GET and a mutation binds POST . "status.report": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "anonymous", "http": { "method": "POST", "path": "/hooks/status", "signature": { "header": "X-Starter-Signature", "secret": "status_webhook" } }, "statement": "INSERT INTO status (component, state, detail, updated_at) VALUES (?1, ?2, COALESCE(?3, ''''), ?4) ON CONFLICT (component) DO UPDATE SET state = excluded.state, detail = excluded.detail, updated_at = excluded.updated_at WHERE status.state IS NOT excluded.state OR status.detail IS NOT excluded.detail", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "component", "type": "string", "maxLength": 40 }, { "source": "input", "key": "state", "type": "string", "maxLength": 12 }, { "source": "input", "key": "detail", "type": "string", "maxLength": 200, "nullable": true }, { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ] } callers lists which proofs the route takes, from signature , key and session . Without it, a signed route takes a signature or a key. An unsigned route on a Private Deployment takes a key or a session. An unsigned route on a Public Deployment answers anyone. A provider''s scheme ( hmac-body , timestamped , svix ) and fields , a JSON path per input, take a webhook as GitHub, Shopify, Stripe or Resend send it. An action whose route does not answer anyone answers only there, never through miniapp.actions.call . kind: call A call action queues one HTTP request that the Kernel sends from its own process, never from the browser. It answers { callId, queued: true } . "status.escalate": { "kind": "call", "access": "authenticated", "method": "POST", "url": "http://inbox.localhost/hooks/events", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "eventId", "type": "string", "maxLength": 100 }, { "source": "input", "key": "severity", "type": "string", "maxLength": 10 }, { "source": "input", "key": "title", "type": "string", "maxLength": 200 } ], "body": { "source": { "value": "starter" }, "id": "eventId", "severity": "severity", "title": "title" }, "auth": { "secret": "inbox_events", "header": "X-Inbox-Signature" }, "then": { "action": "status.escalated", "inputs": { "status": "/status", "error": "/body/error" } } } This is an excerpt of examples/showcase/starter/actions.json . Each body entry is the name of an input or { "value": constant } . auth is { secret, header? } or { key } . then names a mutation that runs with the answer. A URL is https, or http to an app of this installation, and never a private address. The owner reads the outbox with calls_read . Limits in short A project declares at most 100 actions. A statement is at most 20,000 bytes with at most 100 bindings. The limits page has the rest. Read next The Gate and the Kernel Add a Server Action Refuse a write with guard Receive a webhook Pick the smallest piece'),
('/gate-and-kernel', 'The Gate and the Kernel', 'A Capsule meets two checks. The Validation Gate runs in the editor before anything is applied. The Kernel runs the same checks again when the app is released, on every installation, including one unpacked from an export. Why two doors The app has to leave with its rules unchanged. If the editor accepted a statement that the Kernel on another machine refused, the app could not move. So both doors refuse a declaration with the same words, and each rule has one case file that both test suites run. Door Where When Validation Gate src/agent/gate.ts , with the parser in src/kernel/actions.ts Before an agent-proposed change is applied to a Project Kernel kernel-rs/src/main.rs and the modules beside it At release_plan , on every Kernel The Gate checks that the schema is valid, that a schema change comes with a migration that converges, and that every Server Action that runs SQL stays executable against the migrated schema. Shared case files These files live in kernel-rs/src . Both sides parse the same cases. sql-rule-cases.json , for statements action-cases.json , for an action''s name, access, kind, bindings and size http-binding-cases.json , for routes and their callers signal-cases.json , for Signals guard-cases.json , for guards signature-cases.json , for route signatures schedule-cases.json , for schedules call-cases.json , for call actions and connects The query bound is src/lib/query-bound.ts on one side and its port in kernel-rs/src/main.rs on the other (ADR-0009). The LIMIT rule A query that reads rows names a LIMIT at the top level of the statement. This is the same in the editor and on the Kernel that leaves with the app. SELECT id,title FROM items WHERE user_id=?1 LIMIT 50 These cases need no LIMIT . A query with no FROM , which returns one row. A query whose select list holds only totals ( count , sum , avg , min , max ) and has no GROUP BY . These do not satisfy the rule. A LIMIT inside a subquery, a string or a comment. It does not bound the rows the caller receives. :limit , @limit and $limit . They are named parameters, not the clause. A UNION , EXCEPT or INTERSECT with no LIMIT on the whole result. A statement that uses OVER and has no LIMIT . A GROUP BY with no LIMIT , since the result grows with the data. The refusal reads Action <name> reads rows and has no LIMIT. Add a LIMIT. followed by the reason, such as uses OVER, which keeps one result row per stored row, and has no LIMIT. The reasons come from unboundedQueryReason in src/lib/query-bound.ts . What a refusal looks like A refused Capsule answers { code, message, hint } . An MCP tool puts it under structuredContent.error and keeps the sentence in content[0].text . The editor''s Gate gives the same code and sentence. Branch on code , never on message . A code never changes once released. A sentence may. capsule An edit to the app''s files clears it. Change the file the message names and check again. owner The owner clears it outside the files, with the tool the hint names, such as secret_set . No edit clears it. operator Only the installation can clear it. The refusals topic of platform_guide lists every code with its fix. What the Gate does not check The Gate checks the schema and actions.json . A page that names a missing file fails at build or release, not at validate_capsule . The Gate also does not judge a Build Artifact. The builders refuse a bad reference. See Named divergences . Read next Server Actions Limits Named divergences MCP tools and guide topics'),
('/accounts-and-access', 'Accounts and access', 'Three questions stay separate. Who edits the app, who may open it, and who may read a row. Each has its own mechanism, and the host decides none of the last two. Who is who Role What it is Project Owner An authenticated workspace member who edits a Project and administers its data, schema, files, Usage and deployments. Runtime Actor Whoever calls a Deployment at runtime. It is an anonymous visitor, a signed-in app user, or a service holding an API key. App account The account a Runtime Actor of kind app user signs in with on one Deployment. A Runtime Actor''s permissions are evaluated by server-side actions and never imply Project ownership. Do not build a users table of your own that bypasses the Runtime Actor. Use the Signup Policy and invitations. Who may read a row The app decides, in the statement and its bindings. A Server Action binds the caller with { "source": "actor", "field": "id" } or "role" . id is the app user''s ID or the API key''s ID. It is nothing for a visitor and for a schedule''s run. role is owner for the Project Owner''s own app account and member for every other app user. A visitor, a key, a signed request and a schedule''s run have no role, and the binding yields null. "items.list": { "kind": "query", "access": "authenticated", "statement": "SELECT id,title FROM items WHERE user_id=?1 LIMIT 50", "bindings": [{ "source": "actor", "field": "id" }] } Public visibility of a Deployment grants no database access. A signed-out visitor may call only Server Actions declared anonymous , and may list and read files marked public. Guard owner-only actions with a check on the role binding, for example ?n = ''owner'' , instead of a key-only route. Public and private Deployments A Public Deployment opens its shell without signing in. A Private Deployment requires a signed-in Runtime Actor before anything loads or runs. When a request leaves access open for an app with accounts, the guide''s advice to an agent is to propose a private Deployment with the invitation signup mode. Signup Policy The Signup Policy decides who may create an app account on one Deployment. Mode Who may create an account open Anyone. invitation Nobody signs up. An invited person creates the account from the invitation link. closed Nobody signs up. A person the owner invites can still accept the invitation. allowlist Only exact emails or @domain entries, up to 100. A domain entry matches that exact domain, not its subdomains. Entering an address sends no invitation. Public Deployments start Open. Private Deployments start Closed. A first release may name another mode. After that, a republish never changes it, except that republishing a public app as private turns Open into Closed. The Project Owner changes it in the editor''s Auth > Users view, or with signup_policy_set , which needs the manage_users permission. An External Project names its first release''s policy in littlebox.json as project.manifest.signupPolicy . In local verification mode, Littlebox does not verify email addresses. Whoever signs up first with a listed address gets that account. Use Closed for apps that hold private data, or require verification. Invitations An invitation offers one email an account on one Deployment. The link lasts seven days. Accepting it sets the password and activates the account, and the Kernel rechecks the Signup Policy and the account''s state in the same transaction. app_invitations_create sends the email. Call it only when the owner asked for those invitations. Check mail_status first. Inviting needs sending: true . app_invitations_list shows each status. app_invitation_resend sends a new link. app_invitation_revoke ends one. An invitation never changes an active account''s password or resumes a suspended one. A package, a portable export and a restore keep the invitation as history without its link, and the owner resends it. Suspending and resuming An account is pending until it proves control of its email, active, or suspended. Only an active account gets a session. app_user_suspend ends sessions and keeps the account''s ID and rows. app_user_resume returns it to active if it ever verified, otherwise pending . app_users_list shows each account''s state and role. The owner''s account in an app The Project Owner''s own app account has the role owner . Only the owner''s browser session, through app_owner_enter , or an invitation the owner sends to their own email creates it. app_owner_enter returns a link that signs the account in once, within two minutes. Credentials for agents An Agent Credential grants no Runtime Actor identity. Its permissions are read , write , publish , manage_users , export_data and manage_lifecycle . Only manage_users lists, suspends and invites app accounts or changes a Signup Policy. See MCP tools and guide topics . Read next Invite people Server Actions MCP tools and guide topics Concepts'),
('/guides/first-app-from-the-editor', 'Make your first app from the editor', 'Describe an app in plain words, apply the change the Validation Gate lets through, and open the published Deployment URL. Before you start You need to be signed in as the owner. Open Platform access in the editor to create an owner account or sign in. The editor shows Sign in to deploy on the deploy dialog until you do, and brings you back to the app afterwards. Steps On the project list, create a project. The dialog is titled Name this app . Give it a name and pick a starting point under Start with . Each starting point runs as it is. In the conversation panel, describe the app in one sentence. The prompt box reads "Describe the app." Read the proposed change. The files stay as they are until you press Apply . Press Apply . The change goes through the Validation Gate before it reaches the project. The Gate checks that the schema is valid, that a schema change comes with a migration that converges, and that every Server Action that runs SQL still runs against the migrated schema. It also refuses a query that reads rows without a LIMIT. If it refuses, the message names what to change. Try the app in the preview. Ask for changes in the same panel and apply each one. Press Deploy . The dialog is titled Deploy application . It shows "Every query names its LIMIT. It can leave." when nothing blocks the release. Choose who can open the app, Public (anyone can open it) or Private (people with an app account). Then choose who can create an app account. Public Deployments start Open and Private Deployments start Closed. Confirm with the button that names your choice, Deploy public or Deploy private . The dialog then shows Deployment ready with the Deployment URL. Open the Deployment URL and use the app once. That calls a Server Action on the Kernel. Check it worked Observe the result through the same door you wrote through, before you tell anyone it worked. In the editor, the project card shows Open published app once a Deployment exists. Open it and use one feature that reads or writes a row. Over MCP, the read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . It returns the live Deployment and its URL. To see what the Gate''s change did to the files, the read-back for project_sources_save is project_sources_read . What the preview cannot show The editor preview is not a Deployment. It applies no Content Security Policy, so inline handlers and fetches to other origins still run there, and a published app refuses them. It has no HTTP routes, no schedule timer, no notice email, no Usage store, no call outbox and no owner app account. Try those on littlebox dev or on the published Deployment. The Gate checks the schema and actions.json . A page that names a missing file fails at build or release, not at the Gate, so check every path you write. Read next Add a Server Action Invite people Edit the same app''s files with the CLI The Validation Gate and the Kernel Preview and Deployment differences'),
('/guides/edit-files-with-the-cli', 'Edit the app''s files with the CLI', 'Create a folder with init , run it locally with dev , then log in and send it to the Kernel with publish . Before you start init and dev need nothing but a checkout of the repository. To publish you need a Littlebox installation to talk to, and an account on it. login reads LITTLEBOX_EMAIL and LITTLEBOX_PASSWORD . To create the account in the same step, also set LITTLEBOX_INVITATION_CODE , the code the operator issued, or LITTLEBOX_SETUP_KEY for the installation''s first owner. The commands below run from a checkout of the repository. Steps Create the project. init needs an empty folder and no credential. It writes a template and a littlebox.json with a project ID it makes itself. It writes the app private, with signup policy Invitation only. bun run littlebox -- init --dir /tmp/my-app --name ''My app'' Run it locally. dev starts a Development Kernel with its own identities, data and files under .littlebox/dev-data , and republishes each time you save a file. It publishes locally as Open so test accounts can sign up, and it never proxies production. The default port is 8796. bun run littlebox -- dev --dir /tmp/my-app Edit the Capsule files in the folder. Open the URL dev prints and reload after each change. Email, notices included, goes to .littlebox/dev-data/mailbox.jsonl . Optional. Write the Action Contract and, against a test Deployment, check every action''s answers. bun run littlebox -- contract --dir /tmp/my-app bun run littlebox -- conform --url <app URL> --contract /tmp/my-app/action-contract.json conform runs each mutation once, so point it at a test Deployment. Log in. This saves a 24-hour token with read , write and publish in <folder>/.littlebox/credentials.json . Keep .littlebox out of Git. LITTLEBOX_MCP_TOKEN overrides the file. LITTLEBOX_EMAIL=you@example.com LITTLEBOX_PASSWORD=... \ bun run littlebox -- login --dir /tmp/my-app --url http://localhost:4173 Publish. This builds the folder and publishes it with the credential from login , to the installation login saved. The first publish registers the project there and writes the address into littlebox.json . It prints the Deployment URL. bun run littlebox -- publish --dir /tmp/my-app Open the Deployment URL and call one Server Action. Visibility and signup publish deploys the app as private unless littlebox.json sets project.manifest.visibility to "public" . project.manifest.signupPolicy names who may create an app account on the first release. A later publish that names another policy warns and changes nothing. Change it in the editor''s Auth > Users view or with signup_policy_set . Check it worked Do not treat the URL publish printed as proof. Read the Deployment back through MCP, the same door the CLI wrote through. The read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . It shows the live Deployment and its URL. The read-back for project_create is project_inspect as well. Open the Deployment URL, then call one Server Action. actions_invoke is its own read-back, so call a query to see a row you wrote. Decide what your credential may do from session_inspect ''s can map, not from memory of which flag login took. What differs from the editor preview dev runs the same Rust Kernel as a Deployment, so HTTP routes, the schedule timer, notice email, Usage and the call outbox all work there. The editor preview has none of them. The Validation Gate checks the schema and actions.json only, so a page that names a missing file fails at build or publish. Read next Add a Server Action Take the app elsewhere Eject a managed project CLI reference The Capsule'),
('/guides/add-a-server-action', 'Add a Server Action', 'Declare a named query or mutation in actions.json , call it from the page with miniapp.actions.call , and keep SQL on the Kernel. How it works A Server Action is the deployed app''s only database interface. It runs one SQL statement. A query starts with SELECT and a mutation starts with INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE or REPLACE . The browser sends only the action name and its input. The SQL and the typed bindings stay in actions.json on the server. Steps Declare the table in schema.sql . This is from the starter app. CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS notes ( id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, user_id TEXT NOT NULL, title TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (length(trim(title)) BETWEEN 1 AND 120), body TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '''', created_at INTEGER NOT NULL ); Add a mutation to actions.json . Each ?n in the statement takes the binding at position n. A binding comes from the caller''s input , the actor (its id or role ) or the kernel ( now ). Mark an input nullable to bind a missing value as SQL NULL. "notes.add": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "authenticated", "statement": "INSERT INTO notes (user_id, title, body, created_at) VALUES (?1, ?2, COALESCE(?3, ''''), ?4)", "bindings": [ { "source": "actor", "field": "id" }, { "source": "input", "key": "title", "type": "string", "maxLength": 120 }, { "source": "input", "key": "body", "type": "string", "maxLength": 4000, "nullable": true }, { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ] } Add a query that reads the rows back. A query that reads rows names a top-level LIMIT . A total with count , sum , avg , min or max and no GROUP BY does not need one. "attachments.list": { "kind": "query", "access": "authenticated", "statement": "SELECT id, file_id, name, size, public, created_at FROM attachments WHERE user_id = ?1 ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 50", "bindings": [ { "source": "actor", "field": "id" } ] } Choose the access . authenticated needs an app session. anonymous also runs for a signed-out visitor on a public Deployment, and the row it touches is still the app''s decision. Call it from the page. A mutation answers { changes, lastInsertId } . Read the changed rows with a separate query, because publication rejects mutation RETURNING . await miniapp.actions.call("notes.add", { title: "Buy milk" }); const rows = await miniapp.actions.call("notes.list"); Apply the change in the editor, or save the files and run littlebox dev . The Validation Gate and the Kernel refuse a bad statement, name, access, kind or binding with the same words. A project declares at most 100 actions. Check it worked After a save over MCP, the read-back for project_sources_save is project_sources_read . After a release, the read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . Call the action through the door the page uses. On the runtime endpoint that is actions_invoke , and it is its own read-back, so call the query after the mutation and look for the row. Write the Action Contract and read what the Kernel took. bun run littlebox -- contract --dir /tmp/my-app It writes action-contract.json , its schema and action-contract.md from actions.json and schema.sql . The editor preview runs actions against its own local database, not the Deployment''s. It has no Usage store and no owner app account, so check those on littlebox dev or a Deployment. Read next Refuse a write with guard Receive a webhook Server Actions Limits'),
('/guides/refuse-a-write-with-guard', 'Refuse a write with guard', 'Put the app''s rule in a mutation''s WHERE , add a guard sentence, and the caller gets that sentence when the rule says no. How it works When a mutation''s statement changes no row, the Kernel normally answers { changes: 0, lastInsertId: 0 } , the same answer as a call that had nothing to do. A guard turns that answer into a refusal. It is a tool error for miniapp.actions.call and a 403 on the action''s HTTP route. Usage counts it under refused and no Signal is recorded. A guard is 1 to 200 characters on one line. It goes on a mutation only. A query or a signal action that declares one is refused. A scheduled run has no caller, so a guard does not apply to it. Steps Write the rule into the statement, so the statement changes no row when the rule fails. This example changes a row only for the member who owns it, and only when the name is free. Add guard next to kind and access . The sentence is what the person sees, so write it for them. "me.rename": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "authenticated", "guard": "Another member already uses that display name, or it is yours already.", "statement": "UPDATE profiles SET display_name = ?2 WHERE user_id = ?1 AND display_name IS NOT ?2 AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM profiles other WHERE lower(other.display_name) = lower(?2) AND other.user_id <> ?1)", "bindings": [ { "source": "actor", "field": "id" }, { "source": "input", "key": "displayName", "type": "string", "maxLength": 40 } ] } For an owner-only action, bind the actor''s role and test it in the WHERE . The role is owner for the Project Owner''s own app account and member for everyone else. "members.promote": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "authenticated", "guard": "Only the app''s owner can make a member a team admin.", "statement": "UPDATE profiles SET role = ''admin'' WHERE user_id = ?1 AND role = ''member'' AND ?2 = ''owner''", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "userId", "type": "string", "maxLength": 64 }, { "source": "actor", "field": "role" } ] } Show the refusal in the page. miniapp.actions.call rejects with the guard sentence as error.message . try { await miniapp.actions.call("me.rename", { displayName }); } catch (error) { status.textContent = error.message; } Do not add a second check statement. The statement has already run and changed nothing, so nothing rolls back. Check it worked Call the action twice through the same door the page uses, once with input the rule accepts and once with input it refuses. The first answers { changes, lastInsertId } . The second answers your sentence. actions_invoke is its own read-back. Read Usage with usage_read and find the call under refused . After a release, the read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . The editor preview refuses with the guard the same way a Deployment does, so you can try it there. It has no Usage store, so read usage_read on littlebox dev or a Deployment. Read next Add a Server Action Email when a row changes Accounts and access Server Actions'),
('/guides/email-when-a-row-changes', 'Email when a row changes', 'Add notify to a mutation and the Kernel queues a notice when its statement changes a row. How it works A notice goes to the Project Owner, to the calling account, or to the accounts a changed row names. It never goes to an address from the input or from a row. The mutation''s answer does not depend on it. Subject and body are templates that take only {input.<key>} for one of the action''s input bindings, {actor.id} , {kernel.now} , {changes.count} and, in the body, {changes} . Steps Pick the recipient. "owner" is the Project Owner''s account. "actor" is the calling app account, and only once that account has proved its email with a verification, invitation or recovery link. { "column": "<name>" } sends one notice to each verified account whose ID a changed row holds in that column. Add notify to a mutation. This one emails the owner when someone joins a waitlist. "waitlist.join": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "anonymous", "statement": "INSERT INTO waitlist (email, source, joined_at) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3) ON CONFLICT (email) DO NOTHING", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "email", "type": "string", "maxLength": 254 }, { "source": "input", "key": "source", "type": "string", "maxLength": 40 }, { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ], "notify": { "to": "owner", "subject": "New on the waitlist from {input.source}", "body": "{input.email} joined the waitlist.\nThey came from {input.source}." } } To email the person who called, use "to": "actor" . This one confirms an order. "notify": { "to": "actor", "subject": "Your order for {input.print}", "body": "We have your order for {input.print}.\nYour account is {actor.id}." } Keep within the limits. The subject is 1 to 120 characters on one line. The body is 1 to 2000 characters, with line breaks and no other control characters. notify belongs on a mutation, not on a query or a signal action. An address or {input.email} as to is refused. Make sure the installation can send. Call mail_status and look for sending true. Without a mail transport nothing is stored and Usage records no-transport . When no email goes out A notice is recorded only when the statement changed a row. A guard''s refusal, an INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING that stored nothing and a failed statement send nothing. Usage counts each notice under notify with a reason such as queued , no-account ( actor with a visitor or a key) or unverified-email . Check it worked Call the mutation, then read the notice''s state with notices_read and the Deployment''s deploymentId . It lists the app''s notice jobs, newest first, 50 at a time. mail_status counts notice jobs per state under queue.notices . On littlebox dev , the email lands in <folder>/.littlebox/dev-data/mailbox.jsonl and goes to no provider. littlebox events lists it with its recipient, subject and text, and dev serves the same list as a page on the next port. After a release, the read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . Delivered means a mail server took the message, not that a person read it. The editor preview sends no notice email and has no Usage store. Try notify on littlebox dev or on a Deployment. Read next Run work on a schedule Invite people Server Actions Pick the smallest piece'),
('/guides/run-work-on-a-schedule', 'Run work on a schedule', 'Declare schedule on a mutation that takes no input, and the Kernel''s own timer runs it when nobody is looking. How it works A schedule fits work such as releasing a hold that lapsed or closing a day. The Kernel looks once a minute and runs the action at the first look after its period opens. A run has no caller, so an actor binding is null and the guard does not apply. A run takes the Deployment''s data lock and has a 2-second statement budget. Steps Pick the timing. { "every": "15m" } runs on a period from 5m to 24h , counted from 00:00 UTC, so 15m runs at :00, :15, :30 and :45. { "daily": "02:00" } runs once a day at that UTC time. A cron string such as "0 8 * * *" is refused. Write a mutation that binds only kernel , actor or literal values. An input binding is refused, because no caller can supply it. This one closes each day at 00:05 UTC. "days.close": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "authenticated", "schedule": { "daily": "00:05" }, "statement": "INSERT INTO days(user_id, day, habits, done, closed_at) SELECT h.user_id, date(?1 / 1000, ''unixepoch'', ''-1 day''), count(*), count(c.id), ?1 FROM habits h LEFT JOIN checkins c ON c.habit_id = h.id AND c.day = date(?1 / 1000, ''unixepoch'', ''-1 day'') WHERE h.archived_at IS NULL AND h.created_at < (?1 / 86400000) * 86400000 GROUP BY h.user_id ON CONFLICT(user_id, day) DO NOTHING", "bindings": [ { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ] } Make the statement safe to repeat. A run that changes no row had nothing to do and is recorded with changes: 0 . A Kernel that was down for several periods runs the action once when it is back. Read the results with a normal query. Nothing is pushed to a page. A subscribe key is refused, so the page polls a query. "days.recent": { "kind": "query", "access": "authenticated", "statement": "SELECT day, habits, done FROM days WHERE user_id = ?1 ORDER BY day DESC LIMIT 30", "bindings": [{ "source": "actor", "field": "id" }] } Optional. To watch a run without waiting, start the Development Kernel with LITTLEBOX_SCHEDULE_MIN_SECONDS set. Every schedule then runs once every that many seconds. The Kernel takes it only on a loopback address. LITTLEBOX_SCHEDULE_MIN_SECONDS=10 bun run littlebox -- dev --dir /tmp/my-app Publish. Publishing never runs the action. The first period it is seen in counts as done, and a republish that changes the schedule starts again from the next period. Rules worth knowing Only the timer runs it. miniapp.actions.call , actions_invoke and the editor preview refuse it with Action <name> runs only on its schedule. It can also declare an http route, which then runs it. A Deployment in maintenance runs nothing and runs each due action once when it leaves maintenance. A run that changes a row can notify the owner once. A notice to actor records no-account because the timer has no caller. The last period run is kept with Usage, so a restart, a copy or a migration does not run it twice. Check it worked Read the runs with usage_read and the Deployment''s deploymentId , plus from and to dates. It returns the newest 500 scheduled runs in the range with action , at , outcome , changes and, for a failed run, error . Call the query that reads the rows the run wrote, through the page. After a release, the read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . The editor preview has no schedule timer and no Usage store. Try schedules on littlebox dev or on a published Deployment. Read next Email when a row changes Refuse a write with guard Server Actions Preview and Deployment differences'),
('/guides/receive-a-webhook', 'Receive a webhook', 'Declare an http route on an anonymous action, name who may call it, and set the signing secret or issue an API key. How it works An action declared anonymous may declare one HTTP route, a method and a path on its Deployment host. A GET route binds a query and a POST route binds a mutation with a JSON object body. An authenticated action may not declare a route. This is the inbound door. To make the app call another service, see the guide on call actions. The route''s callers list names who may use it, any of "signature" (a service that signs the request), "key" (an API key that names the action) and "session" (a signed-in app user). Without the list, a signed route takes a signature or a key, an unsigned route on a Private Deployment takes a key or a session, and an unsigned route on a Public Deployment answers anyone. Steps Declare the route with a signature. This mutation takes a status report from a service that signs with Littlebox''s own scheme. The secret is named here and its value is never written in actions.json . "status.report": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "anonymous", "http": { "method": "POST", "path": "/hooks/status", "signature": { "header": "X-Starter-Signature", "secret": "status_webhook" } }, "statement": "INSERT INTO status (component, state, detail, updated_at) VALUES (?1, ?2, COALESCE(?3, ''''), ?4) ON CONFLICT (component) DO UPDATE SET state = excluded.state, detail = excluded.detail, updated_at = excluded.updated_at WHERE status.state IS NOT excluded.state OR status.detail IS NOT excluded.detail", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "component", "type": "string", "maxLength": 40 }, { "source": "input", "key": "state", "type": "string", "maxLength": 12 }, { "source": "input", "key": "detail", "type": "string", "maxLength": 200, "nullable": true }, { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ] } For a provider that signs its own way, use a preset and map the payload with fields . The presets are github , shopify , stripe and svix , which Resend also uses. Each fields entry is a JSON path into the body, and the route leaves out body keys no input names. "http": { "method": "POST", "path": "/hooks/github", "signature": { "provider": "github", "secret": "github_webhook" }, "fields": { "id": "$.issue.node_id", "title": "$.issue.title", "repo": "$.repository.full_name" } } Set the secret before you publish. A release that signs with an unset secret is refused and the refusal names each one. The value is 16 to 512 printable characters with no spaces. Use secret_set with projectId , name and value , or set it in the editor''s Auth > Integrations view. Publish, then give the sender the Deployment''s URL plus the route path. The Kernel accepts each signed request once. To let a program of yours call a route without a signature, declare callers as ["key"] . This is the starter app''s route. "http": { "method": "POST", "path": "/api/updates", "callers": ["key"] } Then issue a key that names the action with api_keys_create and deploymentId , name and actions . The key is shown once. The editor''s Auth > Integrations view shows a curl for each route the key names. Test a signed route from a shell. The sender puts t=<unix seconds>,n=<nonce>,v1=<hex HMAC-SHA256> in the declared header. The MAC covers the time, the nonce, the method, the path, the query string and the raw body, joined by newlines. body=''{"id":"A-1","total":12}'' t=$(date +%s) n=$(openssl rand -hex 12) sig=$(printf ''%s\n%s\n%s\n%s\n%s\n%s'' "$t" "$n" POST /hooks/orders "" "$body" | openssl dgst -sha256 -hmac "$SECRET" -hex | sed ''s/^.* //'') curl -X POST "https://orders-1a2b3c.example.net/hooks/orders" \ -H ''Content-Type: application/json'' -H "X-Signature: t=$t,n=$n,v1=$sig" --data-binary "$body" Change the host, the path and the header name to match your route. What the Kernel answers A route answers { result } with the rows or { changes, lastInsertId } , and { error } with a status code. A repeated signature answers 409 replayed-signature . A t more than 300 seconds from the Kernel''s clock answers stale-timestamp . A guard''s refusal answers 403. An action whose route does not answer anyone answers only on that route, never through miniapp.actions.call . Check it worked Send one real or test request, then read the row it wrote through a query action. actions_invoke is its own read-back. After secret_set , the read-back is secrets_list . It shows names, uses and update times, never values. After api_keys_create or api_keys_revoke , the read-back is api_keys_list . After a release, the read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . Usage counts the route by method and path, readable with usage_read . The editor preview has no HTTP routes. Try routes on littlebox dev or on a published Deployment. Read next Call another service Add a Server Action Accounts and access Server Actions'),
('/guides/call-another-service', 'Call another service', 'Declare an action of "kind": "call" and the Kernel sends the request from its own process, retries it and runs a then mutation with the answer. How it works A call action runs no SQL. The page invokes it with miniapp.actions.call , and it answers { callId, queued: true } . The Kernel then sends the request, never the browser, so no secret reaches the page. This is the outbound door. For a service that calls your app, see the guide on webhooks. A URL is https, or http to an app of this installation. It is never a private address, the editor, or a path under /_littlebox , /oauth , /.well-known or /mcp . Steps Declare the call. It takes input bindings only, a method of GET or POST , a url , and a body that maps each body key to an input key or to a constant written { "value": ... } . This is the starter app''s call to its inbox app. "status.escalate": { "kind": "call", "access": "authenticated", "method": "POST", "url": "http://inbox.localhost/hooks/events", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "component", "type": "string", "maxLength": 40 }, { "source": "input", "key": "changedAt", "type": "number" }, { "source": "input", "key": "eventId", "type": "string", "maxLength": 100 }, { "source": "input", "key": "severity", "type": "string", "maxLength": 10 }, { "source": "input", "key": "title", "type": "string", "maxLength": 200 } ], "body": { "source": { "value": "starter" }, "id": "eventId", "severity": "severity", "title": "title" }, "auth": { "secret": "inbox_events", "header": "X-Inbox-Signature" }, "then": { "action": "status.escalated", "inputs": { "component": "/input/component", "changedAt": "/input/changedAt", "status": "/status", "filed": "/body/result/changes", "error": "/body/error", "failure": "/error" } } } For an outside service, use an https:// URL. http:// works only for an app of this installation. Choose auth . { "secret": "<name>", "header": "X-Inbox-Signature" } signs the request with a signing secret, in X-Littlebox-Signature unless header names another. { "key": "<name>" } sends the API key a secret holds as Authorization: Bearer . Set the secret before you publish, with secret_set and "use": "call" . Without "use": "call" the Kernel refuses a value that another of your projects already holds as a route secret. A call secret verifies no route of its own app, so it may repeat the receiving app''s route secret. { "projectId": "<id>", "name": "inbox_events", "value": "<the receiving app''s route secret>", "use": "call" } Declare the then mutation. It runs once when the call ends, delivered or failed, with no actor. It must be a mutation with no HTTP route and no actor binding. Its inputs are read by JSON pointer from /ok , /status , /error , /body and /input . This one records the answer. "status.escalated": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "authenticated", "statement": "INSERT INTO escalations (component, changed_at, status, filed, error, answered_at) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3, ?4, COALESCE(?5, ?6), ?7)", "bindings": [ { "source": "input", "key": "component", "type": "string", "maxLength": 40 }, { "source": "input", "key": "changedAt", "type": "number" }, { "source": "input", "key": "status", "type": "number", "nullable": true }, { "source": "input", "key": "filed", "type": "number", "nullable": true }, { "source": "input", "key": "error", "type": "string", "maxLength": 200, "nullable": true }, { "source": "input", "key": "failure", "type": "string", "maxLength": 40, "nullable": true }, { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ] } Call it from the page like any action. const { callId, queued } = await miniapp.actions.call("status.escalate", { component, changedAt, eventId, severity, title }); Limits 20 calls queued per app in any 60 seconds, unless the installation sets LITTLEBOX_CALLS_PER_MINUTE . 10 seconds per attempt and 64 KiB per answer. 5 attempts for a network failure, a timeout, 408, 425, 429 or a 5xx, waiting 30 seconds, then 1, 2 and 4 minutes. Any other answer is final. A redirect is not followed. A paused app sends no call until its pause ends. Check it worked Read the outbox with calls_read and the Deployment''s deploymentId . It lists calls newest first with status ( pending , delivered or failed ), attempts, URL, error kind and what the then mutation did. Query the table the then mutation wrote. In the starter app, that is status.escalations . After secret_set , the read-back is secrets_list . After a release, the read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . The editor preview answers { callId, queued: true } and sends nothing, and it has no call outbox. Run calls on littlebox dev or on a published Deployment. Pending calls stay on the installation and do not travel in a package. Read next Receive a webhook Add a Server Action Server Actions Preview and Deployment differences'),
('/guides/upload-files', 'Upload files', 'Use miniapp.files for the bytes and a mutation for the row that remembers the file''s ID. How it works The Kernel stores small files for one Deployment. A file is private to the person who uploaded it unless the upload asks for "public" . Large bytes do not belong in a SQLite column. Keep the file''s ID in a row and let the Kernel keep the bytes. 1 byte to 2 MiB per file, and 20 MiB of files per Deployment. An upload needs an app session. A signed-out visitor on a public Deployment can list and read public files only. Only the uploader changes a file''s visibility or deletes it. A deleted file stops counting against the quota. Steps Add a table that holds the file''s ID. This is from the starter app. CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS attachments ( id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, user_id TEXT NOT NULL, file_id TEXT NOT NULL, name TEXT NOT NULL, size INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (size >= 0), public INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (public IN (0, 1)), created_at INTEGER NOT NULL ); Add a mutation that stores the ID next to the actor. "attachments.add": { "kind": "mutation", "access": "authenticated", "statement": "INSERT INTO attachments (user_id, file_id, name, size, created_at) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3, ?4, ?5)", "bindings": [ { "source": "actor", "field": "id" }, { "source": "input", "key": "fileId", "type": "string", "maxLength": 64 }, { "source": "input", "key": "name", "type": "string", "maxLength": 180 }, { "source": "input", "key": "size", "type": "number" }, { "source": "kernel", "field": "now" } ] } Upload from the page, then record the file. Attach the listener in JavaScript, because a published app refuses inline handler attributes. const stored = await miniapp.files.upload(file); // private to the signed-in user await miniapp.actions.call("attachments.add", { fileId: stored.id, name: stored.name, size: stored.size }); Pass { visibility: "public" } only when every visitor of a public app may read the bytes. Show a file with files.url . It returns the path /_littlebox/files/<id> on the app''s own origin, which img , fetch and a link all reach. A Blob URL from files.list or files.upload cannot be fetched again under the page''s policy. image.src = await miniapp.files.url(photo.file_id); Publish or remove a file later with the uploader''s session. The ID and the files.url path stay the same. await miniapp.files.update(draft.id, { visibility: "public" }); await miniapp.files.delete(note.file_id); // { id, deleted: true } If the row''s mutation fails after the upload, delete the file so it does not keep counting against the quota. try { await miniapp.actions.call("attachments.add", { fileId: stored.id, name: stored.name, size: stored.size }); } catch (error) { await miniapp.files.delete(stored.id).catch(() => {}); throw error; } Refusals you may meet authentication-required when nobody is signed in. Transfer size is outside the limit. when the file is empty or over 2 MiB. This deployment has reached its 20 MiB file quota. Only the person who uploaded this file may change or delete it. Check it worked After an upload, the read-back for files_upload is files_list . After files_update or files_delete , it is files_list too. In the page, that is miniapp.files.list() . Open the files.url path in the app and check the file loads. A visitor without a session on a private Deployment gets 401 authentication-required . Anyone else who may not read the file gets 404 File not found. Query the row from step 2 to confirm the ID was stored. The editor preview keeps files in browser storage and never reaches the 20 MiB quota. Published apps send them to the Kernel in chunked, checksummed transfers, so try a real upload on littlebox dev or a Deployment. A package carries the files when the app moves. Read next Add a Server Action Take the app elsewhere Limits Examples'),
('/guides/invite-people', 'Invite people', 'Set a signup policy for the Deployment, then send an invitation to each email that should get an account. How it works The signup policy decides who may create an app account. It is one of Open (anyone), Invitation only (only through an invitation), Closed (nobody signs up, and an invitation the owner sends can still be accepted) or Allowlist (exact emails or @domain entries, an advanced option). Public Deployments start Open and Private Deployments start Closed. A first release may name another policy. A republish never changes it afterwards, except to close an Open app made private. An invitation is the owner''s offer of an account on one Deployment to one email. It is a link valid for seven days. Accepting it sets the password and activates the account. Steps in the editor Open the project and go to Auth > Users. After a first deploy of a Private app with the Invitation only policy, the published state also offers Invite people . Check the signup policy. Change it here if you need to. The change keeps existing accounts working. Invite each person by email. Only invite people the owner asked for, because this sends email. Resend or revoke an invitation from the same view. You can also suspend and resume an app account. Suspension ends its sessions and keeps its rows. Steps over MCP Log in with the manage_users permission. signup_policy_read needs only read . LITTLEBOX_EMAIL=you@example.com LITTLEBOX_PASSWORD=... \ bun run littlebox -- login --dir /tmp/my-app --url http://localhost:4173 --manage-users Set the policy if the app needs another one. Use signup_policy_set with deploymentId and mode of open , invitation , closed or allowlist , plus allowlist entries. Call mail_status . Inviting needs sending true. Without a mail transport, app_invitations_create refuses and creates nothing. Call app_invitations_create with deploymentId , emails and idempotencyKey . It emails each address a link to /_littlebox/invitation#invitation=<token> on the app''s host. Call it only when the owner asked for those invitations. The person opens the link and chooses a password. The page posts it to /_littlebox/auth/accept and signs them in. Use app_invitation_resend to send a new link, which replaces the earlier one. Use app_invitation_revoke to end one. Revoking removes the pending account it created, if nobody used it. Your own app account The Project Owner''s own app account has the role owner . An invitation you send to your own email gives that role. app_owner_enter , for the owner''s browser session only, creates or links the account and returns a link that signs it in once within two minutes. Guard owner-only actions with ?n = ''owner'' on an actor binding of the role . Check it worked After signup_policy_set , the read-back is signup_policy_read . After app_invitations_create , app_invitation_resend or app_invitation_revoke , the read-back is app_invitations_list . It shows each invitation''s status. After app_user_suspend , app_user_resume or app_owner_enter , the read-back is app_users_list . On littlebox dev , the invitation email lands in <folder>/.littlebox/dev-data/mailbox.jsonl , and littlebox events lists it with its link. Decide whether you may call these tools from session_inspect ''s can map. A tool your credential may not call answers Unknown tool or insufficient permission. and names the permission. The editor preview has no owner app account and sends no email, so test invitations on littlebox dev or on a Deployment. A package keeps the invitation as history without its link, and the owner resends it. Read next Take the app elsewhere Refuse a write with guard Accounts and access MCP reference'),
('/guides/take-the-app-elsewhere', 'Take the app elsewhere', 'Export a package to move one app''s accounts, data and rules to another machine, or run a migration when exactly one installation must take the writes. Pick the right move You want Use Why A copy of one app on another machine Package export and import It moves one Deployment''s database, accounts, files and, when present, Usage and Signals. The app to leave and the old one to stop Migration It pauses the origin, then retires it when you confirm, so one installation takes the writes. To move a whole installation Copy A copy moves an installation, not one app. It is operator work, not an app feature. To edit the code outside Littlebox Eject A package moves data, not source authority. A package never moves sessions, email links or live API keys. People sign in again with their passwords, and each API key arrives revoked, so you issue new ones. Signing secrets do not travel either, so set them again on the destination. Export a package Log in with export_data . LITTLEBOX_EMAIL=you@example.com LITTLEBOX_PASSWORD=... \ bun run littlebox -- login --dir /tmp/my-app --url http://localhost:4173 --export-data For a self-hosted kit that includes the live data, run export with --with-data . It refuses to overwrite an archive. Exports with live state hold password hashes and uploaded files, so keep them private. bun run littlebox -- export --dir /tmp/my-app --out /tmp/my-app-with-data.tar --with-data Or over MCP, call package_export with the deploymentId . It returns a manifest and one download per component, database , identity , files and, when the app recorded any, usage . Read each download with transfer_read and its transferId and offset until nextOffset equals size . Check its SHA-256, then call transfer_cancel . Import on the destination Run project_create with the same projectId . Then run secret_set for every secret the app''s routes sign with. A release that signs with an unset secret is refused. For each component, call transfer_begin with size , sha256 , projectId and the component''s name. Call transfer_write for each block, then transfer_complete . Build the folder and upload the release JSON, with initialPackage holding the manifest and a components map of component name to transferId . Then call release_plan and release_apply . An import installs only into a project that has no Deployment yet. The same package imported again returns the first import. Issue new API keys. api_keys_list shows each key revoked at import. Migrate with one writer at a time A migration needs manage_lifecycle and export_data on the origin and manage_lifecycle on the destination. Log in with both flags. bun run littlebox -- login --dir /tmp/my-app --url http://localhost:4173 --export-data --manage-lifecycle Call migration_begin with the origin''s deploymentId . The app pauses at once and shows a maintenance page. It takes no writes, signups, sign-ins, invitations or HTTP routes. The answer holds the package downloads and the next steps. Import on the destination as above, adding the release download as the release payload. The imported app stays in maintenance. Check it with read tools. Call migration_confirm on the origin with operationId and, if the address changed, newUrl . The origin stays retired and shows a link to the new address for seven days. Keep the confirmation it returns. Call migration_activate on the destination with deploymentId and the confirmation . Add resendInvitations to send pending invitations again from the new host. To stop, call migration_cancel with operationId before migration_confirm . It resumes the origin. After a confirm, cancel is refused. Check it worked After package_export , project_export or database_export , the read-back is transfer_read . Verify the size and SHA-256 of every component. After transfer_begin , transfer_write or transfer_complete , the read-back is transfer_status . Bytes in an open transfer are not a Deployment. After release_apply on the destination, the read-back is project_inspect . After any migration_* call, the read-back is migration_status . A service_timeout or interrupted call must be verified the same way before you retry. On the destination, sign in with an existing password and open one row the app owned. The editor preview is not a Deployment, so it has no database to package. Test the whole move on two littlebox dev Kernels or two real installations. The repository''s migration rehearsal runs the procedure on two local Kernels with bun run tests/smoke/migration.ts --port 19460 . Read next Eject Invite people Pick the smallest piece Concepts'),
('/guides/eject', 'Eject a managed project', 'Hand a Managed project''s source to its owner as a folder on disk, while its Deployment, URL, accounts and data stay where they are. What changes Eject is irreversible. The project becomes External. The Kernel records who ejected it and when, refuses every Managed source change from every tab, agent and credential, and keeps accepting Build Artifacts. The Deployment''s URL, accounts, sessions, data, invitations and email continue. Eject exports no data. To edit in the editor again, create a new project. A project you made with littlebox init is already External, and eject_plan refuses it. If you want to move data, use a package instead. Steps in the editor On the project list, find the Take it with you group. It lists each Managed project with an Eject action. Press Eject for the project. The editor downloads the code as a tar and checks it, and only then ejects. The project opens read-only with the commands to publish from outside. Steps with the CLI Log in with manage_lifecycle . The read, write, publish, manage_users and export_data permissions do not include it. LITTLEBOX_EMAIL=you@example.com LITTLEBOX_PASSWORD=... \ bun run littlebox -- login --dir /tmp/ejected --url http://localhost:4173 --manage-lifecycle Run eject into an empty folder, naming the project''s ID. It plans at the revision the server last saved, or at --revision . It downloads the package, checks every file''s SHA-256, writes the files and a littlebox.json , and only then applies. bun run littlebox -- eject --dir /tmp/ejected --project <project id> --url http://localhost:4173 Publish from the folder. The Deployment, its URL and its data stay. bun run littlebox -- publish --dir /tmp/ejected A stale revision is refused before anything downloads. After an interruption, run the same eject command again in the same folder. The receipt is saved in .littlebox/eject-receipt.json . Over MCP Both calls need manage_lifecycle . eject_plan takes projectId and expectedRevision , the saved-sources revision that project_inspect returns as sourcesRevision . It answers a planId . eject_apply takes that planId , checks it is still current and records the Eject. Check it worked The read-back for eject_plan is eject_plan itself. The read-back for eject_apply is project_inspect . It returns the Deployment and its URL, which keeps serving. The project''s source authority now reads external. Open the Deployment URL and use one feature that reads a row. The accounts and rows are unchanged. project_sources_read still returns the frozen sources, marked readOnly: true . After you publish from the folder, the read-back for release_apply is project_inspect . The editor preview cannot show an Eject. It has no Deployment to keep serving, so check on the published app. Read next Edit the app''s files with the CLI Take the app elsewhere CLI reference Concepts'),
('/cli', 'CLI', 'The littlebox command makes an app in a folder, runs it on a Development Kernel, publishes it, and moves it. Run it from the repository root with Bun. bun run littlebox -- <command> [options] bun run littlebox -- help init , dev , build and contract need no credential. Every other command except login reads its credential from <folder>/.littlebox/credentials.json or from LITTLEBOX_MCP_TOKEN . Never put a token in app source. login bun run littlebox -- login --dir ./my-app --url http://localhost:4173 [--manage-users] [--export-data] [--manage-lifecycle] [--verify-from <mailbox.jsonl>] Reads LITTLEBOX_EMAIL and LITTLEBOX_PASSWORD . Add LITTLEBOX_INVITATION_CODE , the code the operator issued, to create the account, or LITTLEBOX_SETUP_KEY for an installation''s first owner. It saves a 24-hour token with read , write and publish . Flag Adds For --manage-users manage_users Inviting app users, suspending, changing the Signup Policy --export-data export_data Exporting live data --manage-lifecycle manage_lifecycle Eject and migration A delegated token cannot widen itself later, so the flags are the only way the CLI gets these permissions. On a Kernel that verifies email, creating the account sends a link. --verify-from reads that link from a development mailbox on this machine and works only with a Kernel on localhost or 127.0.0.1 . init bun run littlebox -- init --dir ./my-app [--name ''My app''] [--template <id>] [--url <Kernel URL>] Writes a template and littlebox.json into an empty folder, without a credential or a Kernel. The templates are the editor''s: tasks , notes (the default), members , form , webhook , digest and gallery . The project is External, and its first publish registers it on the installation. dev bun run littlebox -- dev --dir ./my-app [--port 8796] [--events-port 8797] [--reset] Compiles and starts the Rust Kernel with data under .littlebox/dev-data , with its own identities and no link to production. It publishes the build there, sets the signup policy to Open so test accounts can sign up, and rebuilds when the sources change. Its email, notices included, goes to .littlebox/dev-data/mailbox.jsonl . When the command exits, the Kernel stops and the data stays in the folder. --reset deletes that data first. A Development Kernel with no data starts from the copy pull left, when there is one. pull bun run littlebox -- pull --dir ./my-app [--url <Kernel URL>] Downloads the published app''s database, accounts and files into .littlebox/pull . Needs a credential from login --export-data . Run dev --reset to start the local app from the copy. Nothing travels back to the published app. sql bun run littlebox -- sql --dir ./my-app [--prod] [--url <Kernel URL>] ("<statement>" | --file <file.sql>) Runs SQL on the app''s database through database_execute , on the Development Kernel that dev is running for the folder. That is where to rehearse a schema change on a pulled copy. Only --prod reaches the published Deployment. events bun run littlebox -- events --dir ./my-app [--follow] [--json] [--days 7] [--prod] Lists what the app did besides answer its page: email, calls to other services, scheduled runs, routes and Signals, oldest first. On the Development Kernel an email shows its recipient and text, because nothing is sent. --json prints one object per line, --follow keeps printing, and only --prod reads the published Deployment. While dev runs, the same list is a page on the port after the app''s. build bun run littlebox -- build --dir ./my-app Writes .littlebox/artifact.json . contract bun run littlebox -- contract --dir ./my-app [--out <folder>] Writes action-contract.json , its schema and action-contract.md , from actions.json and schema.sql . publish bun run littlebox -- publish --dir ./my-app [--url <Kernel URL>] Builds and publishes to the installation littlebox.json names, or --url , or the one login saved. Set each signing secret with secret_set first, because a release that signs with an unset secret is refused. On a first release it sends project.manifest.signupPolicy from littlebox.json . conform bun run littlebox -- conform --url <app URL> --contract ./my-app/action-contract.json [--token <app session>] [--key <API key>] [--secret <name>=<value>] [--inputs <file>] Calls every action and checks the answers against the contract. It runs each mutation once, so point it at a test Deployment. export bun run littlebox -- export --dir ./my-app [--out <file>] [--with-data] Writes a self-hosted kit. --with-data adds the live package and needs a credential from login --export-data . eject bun run littlebox -- eject --dir ./empty-folder --project <id> --url <Kernel URL> [--revision <n>] Moves a Managed project''s sources into the folder. Needs login --manage-lifecycle . domain bun run littlebox -- domain --deployment <id> [--set <hostname> | --clear] [--url <Kernel URL>] Shows, sets or clears the app''s custom domain. --set prints the TXT record that proves you control the hostname. Run it again once the record is at your DNS provider. Read next Edit files with the CLI Take the app elsewhere MCP tools and guide topics'),
('/mcp-tools', 'MCP tools and guide topics', 'An agent operates Littlebox over authenticated MCP. The editor calls the same tools with the owner''s session, so a person and an agent meet the same checks. Connect Configure the endpoint http://localhost:4173/mcp in an MCP client. A client with OAuth discovers the server and opens a consent screen in the browser. The flow uses PKCE S256, and the token lasts one hour with no refresh token. A client that supports headers can send a delegated token. Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN OAuth offers read , write , publish and manage_users . It does not offer export_data or manage_lifecycle . After connecting, call platform_describe and projects_list . platform_guide topics platform_guide answers one topic at a time. Start with rules and pieces . Topic What it answers rules The yardstick, the first run, the read-back after every write, and the named divergences between preview and published app. pieces Which Capsule declaration to use for a need. See Pick the smallest piece . credentials Login, flags, OAuth, what each permission allows, and the refusal a missing permission gives. development The CLI cycle with dev , contract and conform . actions The actions.json format and its rules. sdk The window.miniapp modules, rendered from src/sdk/contract.ts . publishing The release steps as MCP calls, access, import, and custom domain. users Signup policy, invitations, accounts, and the owner''s app account. portability Export, import, Eject and migration. refusals Every code a refused Capsule answers, with who fixes it and a one-line remedy. Permissions The catalog of tools depends on the credential. A call to a tool the credential does not list answers Unknown tool or insufficient permission. and names the permission it needs. session_inspect returns can for the scopes the credential holds. Decide from can , not from memory of which flag login took. Permission Tools read projects_list , project_inspect , signup_policy_read , secrets_list , api_keys_list , usage_read , signals_read , calls_read , notices_read , migration_status write project_create , project_sources_save , schema_plan , and database_execute changes publish release_plan , release_apply , secret_set , secret_delete , api_keys_create , api_keys_revoke , and initialSignupPolicy on a first release manage_users signup_policy_set , app_users_list , app_user_suspend , app_user_resume and the app_invitations tools export_data package_export , project_export , database_export , and migration_begin together with manage_lifecycle manage_lifecycle eject_plan , eject_apply and the migration tools A delegated token cannot create tokens or add permissions to itself. Run login again with the flag. Tools by group Group Tools Discovery platform_describe , platform_guide , session_inspect , mail_status , projects_list , project_inspect Projects project_create , project_sources_read , project_sources_save Eject eject_plan , eject_apply Migration migration_begin , migration_confirm , migration_cancel , migration_activate , migration_status Owner schema and data schema_plan , database_execute , storage_test App export package_export , project_export , database_export Publishing release_plan , release_apply Signup policy signup_policy_read , signup_policy_set Signing secrets secrets_list , secret_set , secret_delete API keys api_keys_list , api_keys_create , api_keys_revoke Custom domain domain_read , domain_set , domain_clear Usage and Signals usage_read , signals_read , usage_summary , usage_overview Calls and notices calls_read , notices_read App users and invitations app_users_list , app_user_suspend , app_user_resume , app_invitations_create , app_invitations_list , app_invitation_resend , app_invitation_revoke Transfers transfer_begin , transfer_status , transfer_write , transfer_complete , transfer_read , transfer_cancel Credentials, owner session only tokens_list , token_create , token_revoke App runtime, with an app session auth_session , auth_logout , actions_invoke , files_list , files_upload , files_read , files_update , files_delete App accounts, before sign-in auth_signup , auth_verify , auth_recover , auth_reset The app runtime tools run on an app''s runtime endpoint, /_littlebox/mcp on the app''s host, with an app session. Read back after every write Every tool that writes has a tool that observes its effect through the same door. After a write, call the read-back before retrying or telling the owner it worked. Verify a service_timeout or an interrupted call the same way. Bytes in an open transfer are not a Deployment. transfer_status reads staging, and project_inspect reads a release that consumed them. Write Read-back project_create project_inspect project_sources_save project_sources_read schema_plan schema_plan database_execute database_execute secret_set secrets_list secret_delete secrets_list api_keys_create api_keys_list api_keys_revoke api_keys_list release_plan release_plan release_apply project_inspect signup_policy_set signup_policy_read app_invitations_create app_invitations_list app_invitation_resend app_invitations_list app_invitation_revoke app_invitations_list app_user_suspend app_users_list app_user_resume app_users_list app_owner_enter app_users_list domain_set domain_read domain_clear domain_read transfer_begin transfer_status transfer_write transfer_status transfer_complete transfer_status transfer_cancel transfer_status package_export transfer_read project_export transfer_read database_export transfer_read eject_plan eject_plan eject_apply project_inspect migration_begin migration_status migration_confirm migration_status migration_cancel migration_status migration_activate migration_status token_create tokens_list token_revoke tokens_list auth_logout auth_session auth_signup auth_session auth_verify auth_session auth_recover mail_status auth_reset auth_session files_upload files_list files_update files_list files_delete files_list actions_invoke actions_invoke Read next Pick the smallest piece CLI Accounts and access The Gate and the Kernel'),
('/pieces', 'Pick the smallest piece', 'Pick the smallest piece that does the job. Do not invent a second process, a second database door or a host rule the Capsule can already declare. This is the same table as the pieces topic of platform_guide . The table Need Use Not Page and rows the app owns Capsule + Server Actions Raw SQL from the browser Visitor or member reads or writes a row Named Server Action with access and actor bindings Owner database_execute in the app UI Mutation matched no row guard on that mutation A second check statement Email when a row changes notify on that mutation Browser mail or a Call Action for mail Work when nobody is looking schedule on a no-input mutation External cron unless asked Owner-facing occurrence signal on a mutation, or kind: signal A side table only the agent reads Another service calls this app http on an anonymous action with callers, signature or key A Call Action (that is outbound) This app calls another host kind: call fetch of a secret from the browser Uploaded files miniapp.files Large bytes in a SQLite column Who may create an app account Signup Policy and invitations A homemade users table that bypasses Runtime Actor Take the app elsewhere Package or migration Copy (moves the installation) Debug, or try the next version, on what the published app holds littlebox pull + dev --reset Publishing to find out See the email, calls, scheduled runs and Signals of a local run littlebox events Reading the Kernel''s files by hand Leave managed editing Eject Package (moves data, not source authority) The unit A Deployment folder on the Kernel is the app. It holds the artifact, database, identity, files and Usage. Prefer a declaration inside the Capsule over a new platform resource. Where each piece is explained guard , notify , schedule , signal , http and kind: call are on Server Actions . Signup Policy and invitations are on Accounts and access . Package, migration and Eject are in the take the app elsewhere and eject guides. Read next Server Actions Concepts MCP tools and guide topics'),
('/limits', 'Limits', 'The numbers a Capsule and a Deployment run into. Both the Gate and the Kernel enforce the declaration limits. The Kernel alone enforces the runtime ones. These figures come from docs/mcp.md and CONTEXT.md . When a number matters to a decision, read the section there. Declarations Limit Value Actions per project 100 Action name Lowercase dotted words, at most 100 characters Statement One statement, at most 20,000 bytes Bindings per action At most 100 String input maxLength 1 to 100,000 Guard message 1 to 200 characters on one line Schedule every 5m to 24h Schedule daily A UTC time, 00:00 to 23:59 HTTP route path At most 200 characters Allowlist entries At most 100 Queries Limit Value Rows A top-level LIMIT , or one row, and a 1000-row cap Response size 2 MiB Statement time 2 seconds, checked between SQLite steps LIKE or GLOB pattern 500 bytes App database 256 MiB Requests and rates Limit Value Route request body 64 KiB Route calls without a key or session 30 per 10 seconds per client address Route calls with a user or key 120 per 10 seconds per user or key, shared with actions_invoke Signature clock window 300 seconds Replay window for one signature 600 seconds App login and signup 10 attempts per minute per client address, 5 per minute for one email from one client address Files Limit Value One file 2 MiB Deployment file quota 20 MiB, the sum of the files it holds Files per Deployment 10,000 File name First 180 characters kept Invitations and credentials Limit Value Emails per app_invitations_create 1 to 50 Invitation link Seven days littlebox login token 24 hours OAuth token One hour, no refresh token Transfers and publishing Limit Value Transfer chunk At most 64 KiB, base64 Owner transfers Expire after one hour, eight at a time per owner App user transfers Expire after 10 minutes, two at a time per app user Administrative transfer Up to 32 MiB, or a package component''s limit Publishing payload Must fit in 26 MB, with its artifact in 16 MiB Usage store The store keeps 400 days and at most 100,000 rows of totals, the newest 50,000 Signals and the newest 10,000 scheduled runs. A string property keeps its first 128 bytes. Read next The Gate and the Kernel Server Actions Named divergences'),
('/divergences', 'Named divergences', 'These are known differences between the places an app runs. They are not bugs to paper over. Say them when they matter. Preview and published app The editor preview applies no Content Security Policy. Inline handlers and other-origin fetches can still run there. A published app refuses them. Attach listeners in JavaScript, and keep scripts and fetches on the app''s own origin. <!-- Runs in the preview, refused once published --> <button onclick="save()">Save</button> What the preview lacks The preview has no HTTP routes, schedule timer, notice email, Usage store, call outbox or owner app account. Try those on littlebox dev or on a published Deployment. Gate and publish builders The Validation Gate checks schema and actions.json . A page that names a missing file fails at build or release, not at validate_capsule . Check every path you write. Read next The Gate and the Kernel The Capsule CLI'),
('/examples', 'Example apps', 'The repository holds three sets of example apps. They are the sources the Kernel is tested against, so each one also shows a rule the Kernel enforces. Showcase Six apps in examples/showcase/ . Each has a replay.ts that starts its own Kernel, publishes the app and runs every flow and refusal. examples/showcase/starter , the typical app in one, and the one to copy first. It has a public landing and API, verified open signup, and member, team and admin areas. examples/showcase/inbox , a private operations inbox fed by signed webhooks. examples/showcase/club , a members-only book club with private and shared files. examples/showcase/gallery , a public portfolio with a contact form and a JSON feed. examples/showcase/habits , a personal tracker used from two devices. examples/showcase/booking , a room board with a public view and staff sign-in. bun run examples/showcase/replay-all.ts Product fit Eight apps in examples/product-fit/ . A smoke run publishes all eight on an isolated Kernel and checks them over HTTP and MCP, then opens each one in Chrome. examples/product-fit/launch , an anonymous waitlist with a guard on repeat addresses. examples/product-fit/journal , two accounts with private rows. examples/product-fit/board , a private team board with an allowlist and member and admin permissions. examples/product-fit/portfolio , an authenticated upload and a public gallery. examples/product-fit/poll , anonymous votes with totals. examples/product-fit/feed , anonymous JSON-RPC reads and writes, and a signed ping. examples/product-fit/docs , a multi-page artifact built from pages/ and public/ . examples/product-fit/probe , a contract probe rather than an end-user app. bun run examples/product-fit/smoke.ts To start from one of them, create a new External Project, then copy the example''s sources over the template and keep the generated littlebox.json . bun run littlebox -- init --dir /tmp/my-journal --name "My journal" --url http://localhost:4173 cp -R examples/product-fit/journal/. /tmp/my-journal/ bun run littlebox -- dev --dir /tmp/my-journal --port 8796 Probes Small Capsules in examples/probes/ that decide the cells of the capability matrix no showcase replay proves. Examples are notify (email on an event), schedule (work run by the timer) and calls (a tasks app that escalates to a desk app). examples/probes/README.md lists all ten. bun run examples/probes/replay-all.ts Read next Server Actions First app from the editor Pick the smallest piece');
